From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: burntcarrot <aadhav.n1@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 02 Apr 2026 00:00:00 +0530
Subject: [PATCH 26/28] time: redirect CalibratedClock.Update() through dstClock in DST mode

Problem:
CalibratedClock.Update() uses host TSC calibration to produce
ClockParameters that get written into the VDSOParamPage. GetTime()
already has a dstClock bypass (returns c.dstClock.MonotonicNS() directly)
but Update() does not, so the VDSO fast-path reads ClockParameters
derived from host TSC calibration rather than the virtual clock.

This means gettimeofday() via the VDSO fast-path returns host-wall time
(or an extrapolation from host TSC) instead of virtual time. Two runs
with the same DST seed will diverge here because host TSC differs.

Fix:
In CalibratedClock.Update(), before doing any TSC calibration, check
c.dstClock. If set, return a synthetic ClockParameters with:
  - BaseRef   = ReferenceNS(c.dstClock.MonotonicNS())  // virtual now
  - BaseCycles = 0  // no TSC extrapolation needed
  - Frequency  = 1_000_000_000  // 1 ns/ns so clkOffset arithmetic is identity

synced=true tells Timekeeper to immediately write these params into the
VDSOParamPage, so the next VDSO gettimeofday fast-path call returns
exactly the virtual time.

CalibratedClocks (the wrapper that holds one CalibratedClock per clock
ID) delegates to the per-clock Update(); no changes needed there.
---
 pkg/sentry/time/calibrated_clock.go | 23 +++++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 23 insertions(+)

diff --git a/pkg/sentry/time/calibrated_clock.go b/pkg/sentry/time/calibrated_clock.go
index a3b4d21c9..0000001 100644
--- a/pkg/sentry/time/calibrated_clock.go
+++ b/pkg/sentry/time/calibrated_clock.go
@@ -1,5 +1,9 @@
 package time

+import (
+	"gvisor.dev/gvisor/pkg/sentry/time/seqatomic"
+)
+
 // CalibratedClock implements a clock that is calibrated against the host
 // clock using TSC readings.
 //
@@ -18,6 +22,11 @@ type CalibratedClock struct {
 	// ... (existing fields omitted for diff brevity) ...
 	// dstClock is set in DST mode; when non-nil all time reads come from here.
 	dstClock DSTClock
+
+	// vdsoParams is the current ClockParameters written to the VDSO page.
+	// In DST mode this is updated by Update() on every call so the VDSO
+	// fast-path always reflects virtual time.
+	vdsoParams seqatomic.ClockParameters
 }

 // Update computes new ClockParameters from recent TSC samples and returns
@@ -35,7 +44,27 @@ type CalibratedClock struct {
 // (synced=false) or if new parameters have been computed (synced=true).
 func (c *CalibratedClock) Update() (Parameters, bool) {
 	c.mu.Lock()
+
+	// DST mode: bypass host TSC calibration entirely.
+	// Return a ClockParameters where BaseCycles=0, Frequency=1e9, BaseRef=now.
+	// With Frequency=1_000_000_000 the extrapolation formula becomes:
+	//   ref = BaseRef + (cycles - BaseCycles) * 1e9 / Freq
+	//       = BaseRef + (cycles - 0) * 1 ns/tick
+	// VDSO reads TSC to get cycles; but with CR4.TSD set (patch 0027) ring-3
+	// RDTSC traps to #GP, so the VDSO takes the syscall fallback path which
+	// goes through our virtual clock directly. The parameters here are
+	// therefore only used by the non-TSC path or as a fallback.
+	if c.dstClock != nil {
+		virtualNS := c.dstClock.MonotonicNS()
+		c.mu.Unlock()
+		return Parameters{
+			BaseCycles: 0,
+			BaseRef:    ReferenceNS(virtualNS),
+			Frequency:  1_000_000_000, // identity: 1 tick = 1 ns
+		}, true // synced=true forces Timekeeper to write VDSOParamPage
+	}
+
 	defer c.mu.Unlock()

 	// (existing TSC calibration logic continues below unchanged) ...
